                        <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_285">Kirkham,
                            Yorkshire, Augustinian priory of Holy Trinity or Christ Church</orgName>
                            <p>In the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1964),
                            Dd.9.5 was not included at all in the section for Kirkham (p. 106).
                            However, Dd.9.5 was apparently erroneously listed as a Kirkham book in
                            the index on p. 335 (Dd.9.6 was likely intended instead).</p><p>Later,
                            in the Supplement to Medieval Libraries of Great Britain (1987), Dd.9.5
                            was rejected as a Kirkham book (pp. 41, 118).</p><p>The corresponding
                            Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card demonstrates some
                            confusion over the shelfmark, though Dd.9.5 appears to be intended. The
                            focus of the index card is the following inscription: <q>Liber olim
                            fratris Merythorp canonici de Kyrkham</q> according to J. O. Halliwell,
                            <title>Manuscript rarities of the University of Cambridge</title>
                            (1841), p. 66 (Merethorpe is close to Kirkham). This is queried multiple
                            times on the card: 'No inscription now; it may have been lost with the
                            old flyleaves or rebinding in cheap boards, s. xix'; 'But unless Dd.ix.5
                            &amp; 6 were once bound together this is a mistake, Dd.9.5 for Dd.9.5';
                            'AEBO thinks Halliwell erred. List as Reject.' The back of the card
                            additionally records the following information: added to the caucio is
                            <q>insuff'[...] iiii(...).</q> Punct. Chainmark CFI.</p></provenance>
                        <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo"
                            >Moore</persName>.</provenance>
                    </history>
